  faqs={[
    {q:"Is Boquete safe for expats?", a:"Yes — Boquete consistently ranks among Panama's safest towns. Petty theft exists as in any tourist area, but violent crime is rare. The established expat community and active neighborhood networks mean most people live with minimal security concerns. Basic precautions (not leaving valuables visible, locking your car) are all that's needed."},
    {q:"What does it cost to live in Boquete per month?", a:"A couple can live comfortably in Boquete for $1,500–$2,500/month, including rent ($800–$1,200 for a furnished house), food (fresh local produce is very affordable), and utilities. If you own your home and cook locally, $1,200–$1,800 is realistic. Rent rises quickly if you want panoramic views or a fully-equipped gated property."},
    {q:"Do I need a car to live in Boquete?", a:"A car is strongly recommended. While the town center is walkable, getting to nearby neighborhoods (Volcancito, Alto Quiel) and making the trip to David city for specialist medical care or major shopping requires a vehicle. Taxis are available but limited at night or in outlying areas."},
    {q:"What visa do most retirees use to live in Boquete?", a:"The Pensionado visa is by far the most common — it requires a guaranteed lifetime pension of at least $1,000/month and grants permanent residency with significant discounts on healthcare, flights, and entertainment. The Friendly Nations visa (which requires only a bank account and ties to a friendly-nation country) is another popular option for those under retirement age."},
    {q:"Is there adequate medical care in Boquete?", a:"Boquete has basic clinics and general practitioners. For emergencies or specialist care, most expats go to David city (30–40 minutes away), which has Hospital Chiriquí — a well-regarded regional hospital. Serious cases requiring advanced treatment or surgery are handled in Panama City. Expat health insurance that covers medical evacuation is strongly advised."},
  ]}
